    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the component-library concept.\n\nStep 1 — Define typed text roles.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ntype TextRole =\n  | \"heading1\"\n  | \"heading2\"\n  | \"body\"\n  | \"caption\";\n\ninterface TypographyStyle {\n  fontSize: string;\n  lineHeight: string;\n  fontWeight: number;\n}\n\nconst typography: Record<TextRole, TypographyStyle> = {\n  heading1: {\n    fontSize: \"32px\",\n    lineHeight: \"1.2\",\n    fontWeight: 700,\n  },\n  heading2: {\n    fontSize: \"24px\",\n    lineHeight: \"1.25\",\n    fontWeight: 600,\n  },\n  body: {\n    fontSize: \"16px\",\n    lineHeight: \"1.5\",\n    fontWeight: 400,\n  },\n  caption: {\n    fontSize: \"12px\",\n    lineHeight: \"1.4\",\n    fontWeight: 400,\n  },\n};\n\nfunction getTypography(role: TextRole): TypographyStyle {\n  return typography[role];\n}\n\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / CSS mapping:\n```ts\ntype TextRole =\n  | \"heading1\"\n  | \"heading2\"\n  | \"body\"\n  | \"caption\";\n\nfunction cssForRole(role: TextRole): string {\n  return `var(--text-${role})`;\n}\n```\n\nExample:\nA heading component consumes `heading1` instead of accepting arbitrary `fontSize` values from every caller.\n\nComplexity:\nRole lookup is O(1).\n\nTrade-off:\nA semantic system improves consistency, but product-specific typography needs should be handled intentionally rather than by bypassing the system with random values.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the public contract, consumer impact, accessibility/performance implications, and why the chosen abstraction is justified.",
