    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the component-library concept.\n\nStep 1 — Define a typed icon registry.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ntype IconName = \"search\" | \"close\" | \"check\";\n\ninterface IconDefinition {\n  viewBox: string;\n  path: string;\n}\n\nconst icons: Record<IconName, IconDefinition> = {\n  search: {\n    viewBox: \"0 0 24 24\",\n    path: \"M10 4a6 6 0 1 0 0 12\",\n  },\n  close: {\n    viewBox: \"0 0 24 24\",\n    path: \"M6 6l12 12M18 6L6 18\",\n  },\n  check: {\n    viewBox: \"0 0 24 24\",\n    path: \"M5 12l4 4 10-10\",\n  },\n};\n\nfunction getIcon(name: IconName): IconDefinition {\n  return icons[name];\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / metadata helper:\n```ts\nfunction iconSize(\n  size: \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\",\n): number {\n  return size === \"sm\" ? 16 : size === \"md\" ? 20 : 24;\n}\n```\n\nExample:\n`IconButton` selects `\"close\"` and applies the same size and accessible labeling rules across the entire library.\n\nComplexity:\nRegistry lookup is O(1).\n\nTrade-off:\nA curated icon system improves consistency and bundle control, but maintaining the registry adds asset-governance work.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the public contract, consumer impact, accessibility/performance implications, and why the chosen abstraction is justified.",
