    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the component-library concept.\n\nStep 1 — Model an accessible button contract.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface ButtonA11y {\n  role?: \"button\";\n  disabled: boolean;\n  ariaLabel?: string;\n}\n\nfunction buttonA11y(\n  label: string,\n  disabled: boolean,\n): ButtonA11y {\n  return {\n    role: \"button\",\n    disabled,\n    ariaLabel: label,\n  };\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN / semantic HTML preference:\n```ts\nfunction buttonProps(\n  disabled: boolean,\n): { type: \"button\"; disabled: boolean } {\n  return {\n    type: \"button\",\n    disabled,\n  };\n}\n```\n\nExample:\nPrefer a real `<button>` when button semantics are required instead of recreating keyboard, focus, and disabled behavior on a generic `<div>`.\n\nComplexity:\nAttribute generation is O(1); interaction complexity depends on the component.\n\nTrade-off:\nAccessible primitives require more design and testing work initially, but they reduce repeated accessibility bugs across products.\n\nStep 5 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the public contract, consumer impact, accessibility/performance implications, and why the chosen abstraction is justified.",
